Credential exposure scan — CloudWatch logs

Purpose. Identify plaintext or otherwise usable credentials that have been written into CloudWatch logs, so that anything exposed can be rotated.

Data source. CloudWatch log groups listed in log_groups.txt — the ECS cluster service logs and the Spring Boot application logs. (For the earlier infra-box pass: log group /dna/instance-logs, scoped per instance via the standalone/<instance-id>/ stream prefix.)

Time window. Last 7 days of events per log group.

Method. Each log group’s events for the window were retrieved with aws logs filter-log-events and matched against a fixed set of case-insensitive regular-expression patterns covering infrastructure-service and application credentials. Matches were grouped by log group and category, deduplicated by line with occurrence counts. AWS_RETRY_MODE=adaptive and per-instance/stream-prefix scoping were used to stay within CloudWatch API limits. (State here, accurately, how you actually ran it — e.g. the scanning script, or scoped interactive filter-log-events queries — since whoever validates will likely try to reproduce it.)

What was searched for.

Category	What it catches
Generic password / secret	password/passwd/pwd/passphrase and secret/client_secret with an assigned value
Inline env secrets	PGPASSWORD=, MYSQL_PWD=, any *_PASSWORD/*_SECRET/*_TOKEN=
CLI password flags (shell history)	mysql -p<val>, --password/--pass, curl -u user:pass, redis-cli -a, htpasswd -b, doveadm pw -p, openssl … pass:
Mail (postfix/dovecot)	sasl_username/sasl_password, SMTP AUTH PLAIN/LOGIN base64 blobs
Proxy (squid)	Proxy-Authorization, proxy_auth
HTTP basic auth	Authorization: Basic <base64>
Bearer / JWT	Authorization: Bearer …, JWT tokens (eyJ….eyJ….sig)
URLs with embedded creds	scheme://user:pass@host
Password hashes	$apr1$, $1$/$5$/$6$/$2y$, {SSHA}/{SHA}
SNMP	community/rocommunity/rwcommunity strings
DNS (dnsmasq/BIND)	TSIG / secret "…"
Private keys	-----BEGIN … PRIVATE KEY-----
Spring Boot / Java	spring.*.password/.secret, spring.datasource.password, JDBC URLs with creds (?user=&password= and user:pass@), keystore/truststore passwords, -Djavax.net.ssl.*Password, -D…password/secret/token=, Kafka SASL JAAS PlainLoginModule … password=, OAuth2 client-secret

Known limitations (so results aren’t over-read).

Interactive password prompts (sudo, mysql -p with no value, SSH) are not captured — they’re read from the TTY, not the command line, so they never reach the logs.
Shell-history lines may be absent if HISTCONTROL=ignorespace/ignoreboth was set on the host.
Base64 blobs (AUTH PLAIN, Basic, JWTs) are flagged but not decoded; each needs decoding to confirm it’s a live credential.
Multi-line secrets (PEM keys, stack traces) are detected on their first/header line, not reconstructed in full.
Generic categories intentionally over-capture; findings require a human pass to separate real secrets from benign strings.
A server-side pre-filter, if used, narrows events and can hide credential lines that don’t contain the filter term — runs intended as exhaustive were done with it blank.
